---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'How Hours Apart calculates time, DST and daylight';
const description =
  'A plain-language explanation of the time-zone, daylight-saving and daylight calculations used by Hours Apart, including their limits.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/methodology">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">
        Methodology
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        How Hours Apart works
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Time-zone answers should be explainable. This page describes what the site calculates, where
        the underlying rules come from and where you should verify a result elsewhere.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Local time and daylight saving</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Hours Apart uses the browser&apos;s built-in <span class="nums">Intl</span> time-zone support
        and IANA time-zone names such as <span class="nums">Europe/London</span> and
        <span class="nums"> America/New_York</span>. That lets a selected date be resolved using the
        rules for that date, rather than assuming today&apos;s offset applies all year. The pages
        compare offsets across future dates to identify when a gap changes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">City coordinates and daylight</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Each city has an approximate latitude. The site uses that latitude and the selected date to
        estimate sunrise, sunset and the length of daylight. Latitude matters: places in the same
        time zone can see very different sunrise and sunset times. These values are planning aids,
        not a substitute for local astronomical or official weather information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What can change</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Governments can change daylight-saving rules, sometimes with little notice. Browser and
        operating-system updates carry IANA rule updates, and the site is rebuilt as its content is
        maintained. For a flight, visa deadline, legal filing or other high-stakes commitment, check
        the relevant authority or provider as well as the planned calendar event.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What the planner does not know</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        The meeting planner does not read calendars or free/busy data. Its working-hour bands are
        the hours you choose for each person. It is designed to make a proposed meeting
        understandable before you send an invite, not to decide availability for you.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
